course: dictionary
lesson: 11014
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can mean more than one thing, and the sentence around it tells you which meaning is right. «Sam told his little sister that a swallowed watermelon seed grows a watermelon in your belly.» Here a watermelon means the whole big fruit, the kind that grows from a seed.
explain: «Her plate had a slice of watermelon, sweet and juicy.» Now a watermelon means the juicy flesh you eat. Slices, plates and sweet and juicy are clues that it is food. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: Check your choice by putting the meaning into the sentence. «Swallowing a watermelon seed does not grow a watermelon inside you; the seed simply passes through.» If you read it as the whole fruit growing in your belly, it makes no sense, so the sentence is warning you that the idea is false.
fact: A watermelon is a large round or oval fruit with a hard green skin.
fact: Inside, the sweet, juicy flesh is red or pink, with black seeds, although some watermelons are seedless.
fact: «Watermelon» can also mean the flesh you eat, as in «a slice of watermelon».
fact: Watermelon is about 90 per cent water, which is why it is so juicy and refreshing on a hot day.
fact: Watermelons are a summer fruit, often eaten at picnics and barbecues.
exercise: Sam says a swallowed seed grows a watermelon in your belly. What does watermelon mean here? | A summer picnic game | *A whole large fruit that grows from a seed | A green garden hose | A slice of juicy flesh on a plate
exercise: «Her plate had a slice of watermelon, sweet and juicy.» What does watermelon mean here? | *The juicy flesh you eat | A green garden hose | A huge round stone | The skin of a green fruit only
exercise: What colour is the juicy flesh of a watermelon? | Brown | *Red or pink | Blue | Purple
test: What share of a watermelon is water? | About 25 per cent | About 10 per cent | About 50 per cent | *About 90 per cent
test: Sam's sister believes a swallowed seed grows a watermelon inside her. Is she right? | *No, the seed simply passes through | Yes, but only in summer | Yes, it grows inside you | Only if you chew the seed first
exam: Watermelon is a popular summer fruit at picnics. What is one reason it suits a hot day? | It has no flesh inside | It is a hard metal ball | It is only found in winter | *It is about 90 per cent water
